{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact head 463ec7b9d48f32835dcbc5034892171b27ccd9a4 (request/merge-receipt-discharge, three commits over the merge base 9fbc913e; clean merge onto current main d1ae6ade), primary artifact at internal/workroom/fold.go first and its twenty-three companions, under review request 8b802321, governing request 1d42f753 and implementation promise f305ddf7. All three corrections my ratified verdict 22fb5fb6 required are met, and I found no defect in the code at this head. One thing is missing from the filing, and it stops the merge.\n\nThe artifact set cannot authorize this delivery's own succession. The correction rebuilds the embedded UI, so the merge diff renames internal/service/uidist/assets/index-C3rNnIYs.js to index-BtXL2bAC.js (git diff -M reports R095). I ran the real planner over the live projection (Classify, PlanSuccession, ValidateReach) with main as the pre-head and the merge-tree result as the after side. The plan publishes twenty-five paths, adding internal/service, and it retires two artifacts of mine: the one at the deleted asset path with no successor, and the directory pointer b1c98a63 at internal/service with internal/service as its successor. ValidateReach refuses, because internal/service sits above every reviewed path and the deleted asset is a sibling of the new one, so both lie outside the twenty-four reviewed paths and belong to another actor. The deleted asset is worse than a coverage problem: the fold's cross-author reach needs the planned successor to cover the target path, and a bare retirement covers nothing, so no approval can reach it. I have retired that artifact myself (act 2312f3af, bare, no page cites it), so the remaining fix is one filing: publish a directory artifact at internal/service at this head, cite it in the refiled review request, and I will approve on the same tree. Do not merge on an approval that provably cannot carry the succession.\n\nTwo notes, neither required. The supersede-act half of the actor filter at internal/mergeplan/succession_progress.go line 42 still has no red proof: removing it leaves the mergeplan package and the targeted CLI run green, while the statement half at line 30 goes red. My earlier verdict said the actor filter goes red; that was true of the statement half only, and I correct my own record here. It authorizes nothing new, because another actor's identical supersede would already have been admitted on its own standing. And my earlier note that the recovery branch never calls RequireImplementer was wrong: mergeplan.go line 1418 calls it, at the previous head and in main. Codex was right to decline that one.\n\nArchitecture: layers 5, 6 and 7 are affected, and the correction changes no contract beyond what the five pages already say. Layer 5's contract change (the interpreter freezing the eligible exact-head review set at receipt admission, delivery no longer depending on retirement-plan membership, the projection profile at workroom-fold@24) is unchanged from the head I reviewed before. Removing dischargedBy removed a duplicate statement of the destination rule, not the rule; the destination-keyed delivery index is now the only place that states it. Layer 6 keeps its witness shape. Layer 7 gains the presentation half of a server change that landed at the previous head: internal/app/landing.go stopped producing no_receipt there, and the wire type and the spine now agree with it. The rebuilt embed is byte-identical to a fresh build from this head's source. The five pages are unchanged since 04196681 and still describe this behaviour. The only architecture-adjacent gap is the succession filing above.\n\nSecurity: no new authority, and the removed helper took no load-bearing check with it. dischargedBy recomputed a destination match the delivery key already made, and dropping repository and ref from that key still turns four named tests red. The preview-accuracy gap is closed: each of the three target guards in the recovery branch and the detached-checkout boundary now fails a named assertion when removed, and so do both empty-succession refusals; without the recordMergeSuccession one, a receipt carrying half an authorization pair returned nil and reported success, which is silent acceptance. The receipt-authority half is untouched: an effective ratified exact-candidate approval, an independent implementer, a receipt signed by that implementer and the temporal world rule, with the frozen set a strict subset of what the approval cites. Destination remains part of delivery identity. The UI change renders a server-authored string through React text interpolation, so it opens no injection path. The authority chain is assigned repair: request 1d42f753 from planner to codex, effective; promise f305ddf7, effective; twenty-four artifacts at the exact head on that promise, all live and not stale; my prior verdict ratified by act c6511081. I found no way for an unapproved head, a wrong target or a foreign actor to discharge a commitment.\n\nSimplification: the correction is the simplification I asked for and landed cleanly: one destination rule instead of two, the dead UI state removed rather than worked around. Two small carries: mergeDeliveries.has still reads without a destination while everything around it reads with one (behaviour equals the base; a one-line comment saying the exception is deliberate would finish the job), and two of the five pages rest on source artifacts at a6c7b6f5 for files this correction changed (live, not stale, semantics unmoved; the artifact at this head is the truer basis). Neither needs to hold up the landing.\n\nChecks executed at 463ec7b9 in a fresh detached worktree under my session, bash pipefail, rc 0 unless stated: go build ./...; go vet ./...; go test -count=1 over internal/workroom (7.6 s), internal/mergeplan (4.2 s) and internal/app (62.5 s); go test -count=1 -run 'Merge|Resume|Complete|Succession|Landing' ./cmd/gs/... (128.0 s, including both new recovery tests); go test -race over workroom (9.3 s) and mergeplan (5.0 s); go test ./internal/docset/... (53.0 s); git diff --check over the whole delivery; npm ci then 236 UI tests passing; npm run build, byte-identical to the committed embed; a whole-tree grep for no_receipt, embed included, finding nothing. Sixteen mutants applied one at a time in a scratch copy outside the repository, fourteen compiling: killed were the target pair-coherence guard, the repository-and-ref match, all three target checks together, the detached-target boundary, the Build empty-acts refusal, the recordMergeSuccession empty-shared refusal, the delivery key without repository and ref, the ambiguous match, the retired receipt, both staleness exemptions, the statement actor filter, the whole-suffix append and the UI reason rendering; one survived, the supersede-act actor filter. I also ran the merge-succession simulation described above against the live projection. Carried, not re-run at this head: the full ./cmd/gs/... package (codex reports 371.9 s; I measured 383.5 s serially at the previous head), the targeted recovery race run, and the mutants from my earlier pass this correction does not touch (retirement-plan membership, label resolution, complete reporting). I did not run go test ./... and do not claim it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:25ab386a120499dadab9ce8599eaa6c614aef02b","binding":"assigned","head":"463ec7b9d48f32835dcbc5034892171b27ccd9a4","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:037eeed3e4bb2c3c7d6ba834ec6cefd61b5bfb48\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:f305ddf7e0713bff662d6ed2517f45ceb7a05e4c\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2312f3af05226d0685ad80ed976d939779014017","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}